message.content:
What do we think about this finding?
The two rules disagree about what a non-configured captured remote means:

// resolver — skips it, falls through (fail-soft)
for _, name := range loadCapturedSyncRemotes(ctx) {
    if isConfiguredRemote(ctx, name) { return ... }
}

// capture — only checks non-empty
if len(loadCapturedSyncRemotes(ctx)) > 0 { return }

git remote rename fork myfork and you're stuck: the election falls back to origin (source: default), but capture returns early forever because the file is still non-empty. myfork can never be captured, checkpoints route to origin silently, and nothing tells you. I confirmed nothing else writes or clears that file — saveCapturedSyncRemotes has exactly one caller — and the PR body notes entire clean deliberately leaves it, so the only recovery is rm by hand. Fix is to apply the resolver's isConfiguredRemote filter to the sticky check too.In other words if we:

add new remote
push for the first time to that remote
we track that remote to push checkpoints there
we rename/delete that remote
we don't push anymore checkpoints
is it something we want to track ? (edited)

